#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fetch-engine-assets - the §6.1.3 checksum-verified cache-MISS populate path (P4.28).

`stage-engines` (P4.27) reads the RESTORED engine-asset cache and never touches the network.
This is the other half: when `actions/cache` misses a `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`
key, this script populates it - download the pinned upstream asset, verify its SHA-256 against
the `engines.lock` row's `asset_sha256`, unpack it, and publish the entry in the exact directory
shape `stage-engines.select_cache_entry` looks for. It is the ONLY sanctioned network actor at
package time (§6.1.3's "never the live network at package time" binds `stage-engines`, not this
populate path), and it runs only on a miss.

A CACHE ENTRY IS A GROUP, NOT A ROW `[DECIDED - owner adjudication 2026-09-01, the (A') ruling
on the P4.28 escalation]`. `ffmpeg`, `ffprobe` and `libmp3lame.so` are three §3.7.2 rows with
three ids, three versions and three staged-byte hashes - and ONE download. So the key is built
from the row's `cache_engine`/`cache_version` (defaulting to `id`/`version`), several rows
collapse into one fetch, and the §3.7.2 invariant "(cache_engine, triple) has exactly one
(cache_version, upstream_url, asset_sha256)" is what makes that collapse safe. Keying the group
on the row's own `version` instead would report `libmp3lame` 3.100 inside the FFmpeg 7.1 entry
as an ambiguous entry - a hard failure fired at a correct manifest.

WHAT THE PIN IS A HASH OF. `sha256` is of the STAGED bytes (`RowKind::StagedArtifact`), which
for an archive asset is a file INSIDE the download - so verifying the download against it can
never succeed. The download's own pin is `asset_sha256`, the prebuilt twin of
`from_source.tarball_sha256`, required iff `acquisition = "prebuilt"`. Where the download IS the
staged artifact (a bare binary, no container) the two are equal; that is the degenerate case,
not the rule. Verifying against the wrong one fails as a FALSE poisoned-upstream alarm, whose
obvious "fix" - re-pinning `sha256` to the archive hash - would silently destroy G37's
staged-byte verify.

THE EGRESS BOUND IS THE LOAD-BEARING PROPERTY. Every URL this script opens is READ FROM the
L(-1)-caged `src-tauri/engines.lock` - never from argv, never from an environment variable,
never assembled from a template. `--engine` selects a GROUP; it cannot introduce a host, and
there is deliberately no flag that points the reader at another manifest. So WHERE this script
may connect is L(-1) data policed by the P4.56.3 origin allow-list gate, not script code.
REDIRECTS ARE PART OF THAT BOUND: `urllib` follows them by default and its own policy admits
`http` and `ftp` and any host, so every hop is re-checked here against the same scheme pin and
an allow-list of origins - without which a compromised allow-listed host could 302 the fetch to
plaintext on a host the allow-list never approved. Until P4.56.3's committed per-engine origin
allow-list exists, the allow-list IS the set of hosts the row itself names. THAT INTERIM SET
IS PROVENANCE, NOT TRANSPORT, and the two are not the same: a GitHub release asset 302s to
`objects.githubusercontent.com`, which no row names, so a row pinned at a redirect-to-CDN
source (BtbN's FFmpeg builds, most SourceForge mirrors) cannot be fetched until P4.56.3's
list carries a per-engine TRANSPORT-origin set alongside the corroboration one. Recorded on
that box; widening `corroboration_urls` to carry a CDN would corrupt the field's meaning,
since P4.56.3 reads it as a provenance property (the two on INDEPENDENT origins).

NOTHING IS PUBLISHED BEFORE ITS HASH VERIFIES. The asset is fetched into memory, hashed, and
compared BEFORE a single byte reaches the cache root; the entry is then built inside a private
temp directory and moved into place with one `os.replace`, so a crash or a failed verify leaves
NO partial entry behind - the same either-fully-succeeds-or-leaves-nothing discipline §2.0
imposes on conversion output. A verified entry that already exists is a cache hit: nothing is
re-fetched.

WHY IT UNPACKS. §6.1.3 says "each cache entry stores the verified per-triple binary tree", and
`stage-engines` resolves members INSIDE an entry directory (`_member_path`). A populate path
that dropped the raw archive would produce a shape the consumer cannot read, so a cache MISS
would behave differently from a cache HIT - the exact asymmetry that makes a fallback path rot
undetected. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.28] So fetch -> verify -> unpack -> publish is one
indivisible act. Extraction is confined: tar goes through the stdlib `data` filter (the Python
3.12 floor makes it available and it models more than the pre-check does - device nodes, setuid
bits, absolute links), zip members are validated before extraction, and EVERY member path -
including the bare-asset filename derived from the URL - is checked against the entry root
regardless of what the archive or the URL claims.

SCOPE (P4.28's Loop half - the generic mechanism). `src-tauri/engines.lock` is the L(-1) row set
and it is an EMPTY skeleton today, so a real run is a target-absent no-op that says so - the same
skip-with-notice posture `stage-engines` holds with no `STAGED_ENGINES` rows. The MECHANISM below
is live and exercised by --selftest. Deliberately NOT here: the SHA-pinned `actions/cache`
restore/save wiring and the thin workflow step that invokes this script (`.github/**`, L(-1) -
the Co-Pilot-landed owner-acked tail this box declares), the independent armed canary
`scripts/gate-selftests/g24-fetch-engine-assets.py` which plants the off-`engines.lock`-URL
positive from OUTSIDE this file (`scripts/gate-selftests/**`, L(-1), same tail), the G37
restore-re-verify step the G56 workflow-lint sub-rule requires between the restore and
`stage-engines`, and the from-source CI-compile populate path, which is P4.28.1's distinct box
(this script handles `acquisition = "prebuilt"` rows only and REFUSES the rest, rather than
silently treating a build-output pin as a download).

[Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.28] --selftest lives IN this file, for the P4.27 reason recorded
verbatim in `stage-engines`: the repo's only Python-test runner is `run-gate-selftests` over the
L(-1) `scripts/gate-selftests/`, which the autonomous Build-Loop does not author, and shipping
build tooling with no committed test would be worse. The suite is fixture-driven and NEVER opens
a socket - the one network call sits behind an injected `opener` seam the fixtures replace, and
the REAL opener carries the scheme/host guard itself so it is closed by construction rather than
by its caller. The suite is exception-guarded: an unhandled raise is a NAMED failing leg, never a
dead tally. The record types are `NamedTuple`s, not `@dataclass`es, for the reason `stage-engines`
records: the `g24-*` canary idiom (`SourceFileLoader` + `module_from_spec` with NO `sys.modules`
entry) cannot construct a dataclass, and the tool must be importable under the ESTABLISHED idiom.

Usage:
  fetch-engine-assets --target TRIPLE (--engine GROUP | --all) [--cache-root DIR] [--root DIR]
  fetch-engine-assets --check [...]      # report what WOULD be fetched, write nothing
  fetch-engine-assets --selftest         # the tool's own fixture-driven regression suite

stdlib-only. Exit: 0 = populated (or nothing to do); 1 = a fetch/verify/unpack violation;
2 = a structural prerequisite is unusable (missing lock file, unresolvable target).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import http.client
import io
import os
import platform
import re
import shutil
import sys
import tarfile
import tempfile
import tomllib
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
import zipfile
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable, NamedTuple

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]

# The L(-1) manifest this script reads its rows - and therefore its egress targets - from. There is
# deliberately NO flag that overrides this: an alternate manifest would be an argv-supplied set of
# hosts, which is precisely the bound the module docstring claims does not exist.
LOCK_REL = "src-tauri/engines.lock"

# Kept identical to `stage-engines`: the cache root both halves agree on.
DEFAULT_CACHE_REL = ".engine-cache"

# §3.4.5's v1 target triples. The THIRD copy of this set (`src-tauri/src/engines/lock.rs` holds the
# schema-side one, `scripts/stage-engines` the staging-side one) with no mechanical drift catcher;
# a row naming anything else is a lock error, not a fetch this script should attempt.
V1_TARGET_TRIPLES = (
    "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    "aarch64-apple-darwin",
    "x86_64-apple-darwin",
    "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
)

# The only scheme this script will open, on the first request AND on every redirect hop. Not a
# policy knob: `file://` would turn the populate path into an arbitrary-local-file-read primitive
# (§0.11 T9b), and `http://` would strip the transport authentication the pinned hash cannot
# replace on its own - a hash proves WHAT arrived, never that the host was the pinned one.
ALLOWED_URL_SCHEME = "https"

# How long one read may block. Without it a hung upstream holds the CI job until the workflow
# timeout, with no diagnostic naming the URL.
FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 120

# A refusal to buffer an implausibly large asset in memory. The largest v1 engine payload (a
# LibreOffice program tree) is comfortably under this; the bound exists so a hostile or mis-pinned
# URL cannot drive the runner into swap before the hash is ever checked.
MAX_ASSET_BYTES = 1_536 * 1024 * 1024

_SHA256_HEX_LEN = 64

# A cache-key token becomes a PATH COMPONENT (`<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>` is a
# directory name under the cache root), so it is untrusted input in exactly the way an archive
# member name is. `engines.lock` is L(-1) data, but the whole posture of this script is that a
# MIS-pinned row must not be able to reach outside its lane - and a stray `/` or `..` in a
# hand-edited version string would otherwise put a verified archive somewhere else entirely.
_SAFE_TOKEN = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._+-]*$")

_HOST_TRIPLES = {
    ("Windows", "AMD64"): "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    ("Windows", "x86_64"): "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    ("Darwin", "arm64"): "aarch64-apple-darwin",
    ("Darwin", "x86_64"): "x86_64-apple-darwin",
    ("Linux", "x86_64"): "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
}


class FetchError(Exception):
    """A fetch/verify/unpack violation - exit 1."""


class StructuralError(Exception):
    """A prerequisite that makes the run impossible - exit 2."""


class _AlreadyPublished(Exception):
    """Not a failure: a concurrent populate published this entry first, so ours was discarded.

    Carried as a control-flow signal rather than a bare return so the caller REPORTS the distinct
    outcome; reporting it as a populate would claim this run wrote bytes that another run wrote.
    """

    def __init__(self, digest: str) -> None:
        super().__init__(digest)
        self.digest = digest


class LockRow(NamedTuple):
    """One `engines.lock` `[[engine]]` row, reduced to what the populate path needs.

    The AUTHORITATIVE validator is `crate::engines::lock` (P4.56.1, Rust); this reader deliberately
    re-checks only the fields it is about to ACT on, so the two cannot disagree about a field this
    script never reads. Everything it does read, it fails closed on.
    """

    id: str
    version: str
    triples: tuple[str, ...]
    kind: str
    acquisition: str
    upstream_url: str
    sha256: str
    asset_sha256: str
    cache_engine: str
    cache_version: str
    corroboration_urls: tuple[str, ...]


class FetchPlan(NamedTuple):
    """One §6.1.3 cache ENTRY - the unit this script actually fetches.

    Several rows collapse into one plan: an entry is one download, and `member_ids` records which
    artifacts ship out of it so the report names them rather than the key alone.
    """

    entry_name: str
    triple: str
    cache_engine: str
    upstream_url: str
    asset_sha256: str
    member_ids: tuple[str, ...]
    allowed_hosts: frozenset[str]
    cached: bool


def fail(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[fetch-engine-assets] FAIL: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def note(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[fetch-engine-assets] {msg}")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Target resolution - the same contract `stage-engines` exposes, so both halves of §6.1.3 agree
# on what a triple is.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def host_target_triple() -> str | None:
    return _HOST_TRIPLES.get((platform.system(), platform.machine()))


def resolve_target(explicit: str | None) -> str:
    if explicit is not None:
        if explicit not in V1_TARGET_TRIPLES:
            raise StructuralError(
                f"unknown target triple '{explicit}' - §3.4.5 declares exactly "
                f"{', '.join(V1_TARGET_TRIPLES)}"
            )
        return explicit
    host = host_target_triple()
    if host is None:
        raise StructuralError(
            f"cannot resolve a target triple for this host "
            f"({platform.system()}/{platform.machine()}) - pass --target explicitly"
        )
    return host


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lock reader. Every egress target this script has originates HERE.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _require_str(raw: dict, field: str, where: str) -> str:
    value = raw.get(field)
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
        raise StructuralError(
            f"{where}: `{field}` is missing or not a non-empty string - the manifest must satisfy "
            "the §3.7.2 schema (`crate::engines::lock` is its authoritative validator)"
        )
    return value.strip()


def _optional_str(raw: dict, field: str, fallback: str, where: str) -> str:
    """A group token: absent means "default to the artifact's own", blank means a hand-edit slip."""
    if field not in raw:
        return assert_safe_token(fallback, field, where)
    value = raw[field]
    if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():
        raise StructuralError(f"{where}: `{field}` is declared but blank")
    return assert_safe_token(value.strip(), field, where)


def assert_safe_token(token: str, field: str, where: str) -> str:
    """A cache-key token must be usable as ONE path component.

    Checked here rather than trusted from the manifest: this token is concatenated into a
    directory name under the cache root, so a `/`, a `\\` or a `..` would relocate a verified
    archive out of its lane. `crate::engines::lock` enforces the same grammar on the schema side.
    """
    if not _SAFE_TOKEN.match(token):
        raise StructuralError(
            f"{where}: `{field}` value {token!r} is not usable as a cache-key path component "
            "(letters, digits, and . _ + - only, not starting with a dot)"
        )
    return token


def parse_lock(text: str, *, where: str = LOCK_REL) -> tuple[LockRow, ...]:
    """Parse `engines.lock` into the rows the populate path can act on.

    An empty (comment-only) manifest is a VALID container that yields no rows - the P4.56.1
    skeleton shape - not an error.
    """
    try:
        doc = tomllib.loads(text)
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as exc:
        raise StructuralError(f"{where}: not valid TOML - {exc}") from exc

    raw_rows = doc.get("engine", [])
    if not isinstance(raw_rows, list):
        raise StructuralError(f"{where}: `engine` must be an array of tables")

    rows: list[LockRow] = []
    for index, raw in enumerate(raw_rows):
        if not isinstance(raw, dict):
            raise StructuralError(f"{where}: `engine[{index}]` is not a table")
        seat = f"{where}: engine[{index}]"
        triples = raw.get("triples")
        if not isinstance(triples, list) or not triples:
            raise StructuralError(f"{seat}: `triples` must be a non-empty array")
        for triple in triples:
            if not isinstance(triple, str) or triple not in V1_TARGET_TRIPLES:
                raise StructuralError(f"{seat}: '{triple}' is not one of the §3.4.5 target triples")
        identity = _require_str(raw, "id", seat)
        version = _require_str(raw, "version", seat)
        acquisition = _require_str(raw, "acquisition", seat)
        corroboration = raw.get("corroboration_urls", [])
        if not isinstance(corroboration, list):
            raise StructuralError(f"{seat}: `corroboration_urls` must be an array")
        # `asset_sha256` is required iff prebuilt; the Rust validator is the authority, but this
        # reader would otherwise fetch with nothing to verify the download against.
        asset = ""
        if acquisition == "prebuilt":
            asset = _require_str(raw, "asset_sha256", seat)
        rows.append(
            LockRow(
                id=identity,
                version=version,
                triples=tuple(triples),
                kind=_require_str(raw, "kind", seat),
                acquisition=acquisition,
                upstream_url=_require_str(raw, "upstream_url", seat),
                sha256=_require_str(raw, "sha256", seat),
                asset_sha256=asset,
                cache_engine=_optional_str(raw, "cache_engine", identity, seat),
                cache_version=_optional_str(raw, "cache_version", version, seat),
                corroboration_urls=tuple(
                    url.strip() for url in corroboration if isinstance(url, str) and url.strip()
                ),
            )
        )
    return tuple(rows)


def load_lock(lock_path: Path) -> tuple[LockRow, ...]:
    if not lock_path.is_file():
        raise StructuralError(f"missing engine manifest: {lock_path} (§3.7.2)")
    return parse_lock(lock_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"), where=str(lock_path))


def is_fetchable(row: LockRow) -> bool:
    """Only a PREBUILT staged artifact is downloadable.

    A `sub-component` row's hash anchors a pinned SOURCE, not a cache artifact (§3.7.2 item 4),
    and a `from-source` row's staged hash is OUR OWN build output - a "build-output stability
    check, NOT a provenance anchor" (§3.8) whose populate path is the P4.28.1 CI compile.
    Downloading either would be verifying a hash against bytes it was never a hash OF.
    """
    return row.kind == "staged-artifact" and row.acquisition == "prebuilt"


def refusal_reason(row: LockRow) -> str:
    if row.kind != "staged-artifact":
        return (
            f"row kind '{row.kind}' is not a staged artifact - a §3.7.2 sub-component row's hash "
            "anchors a pinned source, which this path cannot download"
        )
    return (
        f"acquisition '{row.acquisition}' is not 'prebuilt' - a from-source pin is verified "
        "against our own CI build output (§3.8), and its populate path is P4.28.1's compile"
    )


def cache_entry_name(row: LockRow, triple: str) -> str:
    """The §6.1.3 GROUP key, byte-identical to `EngineRow::cache_key` and to what
    `stage-engines.cache_entry_matches` parses back out."""
    return f"{row.cache_engine}-{row.cache_version}-{triple}"


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Grouping: several rows, one entry.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def group_plans(
    rows: tuple[LockRow, ...], triple: str, cache_root: Path, skipped: list[str] | None = None
) -> list[FetchPlan]:
    """Collapse the fetchable rows for one triple into one plan per §6.1.3 cache entry.

    The §3.7.2 group invariant is enforced HERE too, not only in the Rust validator: this script
    can be pointed at a manifest edit that has not been through `validate()` yet, and publishing
    two different downloads under one key is the failure the invariant exists to prevent.

    `skipped`, when given, collects a human-readable reason for every row this path declines, so
    the refusal is observable rather than an unexplained absence from the plan list.
    """
    if skipped is None:
        skipped = []
    ordered: list[str] = []
    by_entry: dict[str, list[LockRow]] = {}
    for row in rows:
        if triple not in row.triples:
            continue
        if not is_fetchable(row):
            # Surfaced rather than silently skipped: the module docstring claims this path REFUSES
            # a from-source or sub-component row, and a refusal nobody can observe is not one.
            skipped.append(f"{row.id}: {refusal_reason(row)}")
            continue
        name = cache_entry_name(row, triple)
        if name not in by_entry:
            by_entry[name] = []
            ordered.append(name)
        by_entry[name].append(row)

    plans: list[FetchPlan] = []
    for name in ordered:
        members = by_entry[name]
        first = members[0]
        for other in members[1:]:
            for field, mine, theirs in (
                ("upstream_url", other.upstream_url, first.upstream_url),
                ("asset_sha256", other.asset_sha256, first.asset_sha256),
            ):
                if mine != theirs:
                    raise FetchError(
                        f"ambiguous cache entry '{name}': rows '{first.id}' and '{other.id}' "
                        f"disagree on `{field}`. One entry is ONE download, so publishing it "
                        "would have to pick a side (§3.7.2 group invariant)"
                    )
        hosts = {urllib.parse.urlsplit(first.upstream_url).netloc}
        hosts.update(urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).netloc for url in first.corroboration_urls)
        plans.append(
            FetchPlan(
                entry_name=name,
                triple=triple,
                cache_engine=first.cache_engine,
                upstream_url=first.upstream_url,
                asset_sha256=first.asset_sha256,
                member_ids=tuple(row.id for row in members),
                allowed_hosts=frozenset(host for host in hosts if host),
                cached=(cache_root / name).is_dir(),
            )
        )
    return plans


def select_plans(
    rows: tuple[LockRow, ...],
    engine: str | None,
    triple: str,
    cache_root: Path,
    skipped: list[str] | None = None,
) -> list[FetchPlan]:
    plans = group_plans(rows, triple, cache_root, skipped)
    if engine is None:
        return plans
    chosen = [plan for plan in plans if plan.cache_engine == engine]
    if not chosen:
        raise StructuralError(
            f"no fetchable `engines.lock` cache group '{engine}' at {triple} - this script can "
            "only fetch what the manifest pins (the egress bound, §3.8)"
        )
    return chosen


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Egress: the only place a socket is opened.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def assert_fetchable_url(url: str, allowed_hosts: frozenset[str] | None = None) -> None:
    """The scheme/host pin, applied to the pinned URL AND to every redirect hop.

    A row is L(-1) data, but a MIS-pinned row - or a compromised allow-listed host answering with
    a 302 - must still not be able to turn this script into a local-file reader, a plaintext
    downloader, or a client of a host the allow-list never approved.
    """
    parsed = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url)
    if parsed.scheme != ALLOWED_URL_SCHEME:
        raise FetchError(
            f"refusing URL scheme '{parsed.scheme or '(none)'}' for {url!r} - only "
            f"{ALLOWED_URL_SCHEME} is permitted (a file:// pin would make this an "
            "arbitrary-local-file-read primitive, §0.11 T9b)"
        )
    if not parsed.netloc:
        raise FetchError(f"refusing URL with no host: {url!r}")
    if allowed_hosts is not None and parsed.netloc not in allowed_hosts:
        raise FetchError(
            f"refusing host '{parsed.netloc}' for {url!r} - not an origin this row names "
            f"({', '.join(sorted(allowed_hosts)) or 'none'}). P4.56.3's committed per-engine "
            "origin allow-list is what widens this set; a redirect cannot."
        )


class _PinnedRedirectHandler(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    """Re-applies the scheme/host pin to every hop.

    `urllib`'s own redirect policy admits `http`, `https` and `ftp` to ANY host, so without this
    the pinned URL is the only one ever checked and the server chooses every one after it.
    """

    def __init__(self, allowed_hosts: frozenset[str]) -> None:
        super().__init__()
        self._allowed = allowed_hosts

    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):
        assert_fetchable_url(newurl, self._allowed)
        return super().redirect_request(req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl)


def build_pinned_opener(allowed_hosts: frozenset[str]) -> urllib.request.OpenerDirector:
    """The opener the real fetch uses, with the per-hop pin installed.

    Factored out so a test can assert the handler actually REACHES the opener: the pin is one
    `build_opener` argument, and losing it is a silent downgrade to `urllib`'s own policy, which
    admits `http`, `ftp` and any host on a redirect.
    """
    return urllib.request.build_opener(_PinnedRedirectHandler(allowed_hosts))


def read_capped(response, url: str) -> bytes:
    """Read a response body, refusing to buffer past MAX_ASSET_BYTES.

    Separate from `http_opener` so the ceiling is exercisable without a socket: the check has to
    live INSIDE the loop (refusing mid-stream), and a regression that moves it after the read
    would defeat the whole point of the bound.
    """
    buffer = io.BytesIO()
    while True:
        chunk = response.read(1024 * 1024)
        if not chunk:
            break
        buffer.write(chunk)
        if buffer.tell() > MAX_ASSET_BYTES:
            raise FetchError(
                f"asset exceeds the {MAX_ASSET_BYTES} byte ceiling before its hash could be "
                f"checked: {url}"
            )
    return buffer.getvalue()


def http_opener(url: str, allowed_hosts: frozenset[str] | None = None) -> bytes:
    """The real network seam - guarded by construction, not by its caller.

    The fixtures replace this wholesale (see the module docstring); the guard lives HERE so that
    calling the real opener directly cannot bypass it.
    """
    assert_fetchable_url(url, allowed_hosts)
    hops = allowed_hosts if allowed_hosts is not None else frozenset()
    request = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"User-Agent": "convertia-fetch-engine-assets"})
    with build_pinned_opener(hops).open(request, timeout=FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) as response:
        return read_capped(response, url)


def fetch_asset(
    url: str,
    *,
    opener: Callable[..., bytes],
    allowed_hosts: frozenset[str] | None = None,
) -> bytes:
    assert_fetchable_url(url, allowed_hosts)
    try:
        data = opener(url, allowed_hosts)
    except (urllib.error.URLError, http.client.HTTPException, OSError) as exc:
        raise FetchError(f"download failed for {url}: {exc}") from exc
    if len(data) > MAX_ASSET_BYTES:
        raise FetchError(
            f"asset exceeds the {MAX_ASSET_BYTES} byte ceiling before its hash could be checked: "
            f"{url}"
        )
    if not data:
        raise FetchError(f"download produced zero bytes: {url}")
    return data


def verify_sha256(data: bytes, expected: str, *, url: str) -> str:
    """Verify BEFORE anything is written. Returns the actual digest for the caller to report."""
    want = expected.strip().lower()
    if len(want) != _SHA256_HEX_LEN or any(c not in "0123456789abcdef" for c in want):
        raise FetchError(
            f"malformed `asset_sha256` pin {expected!r} - a §3.7.2 row carries 64 lowercase hex "
            "characters"
        )
    actual = hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()
    if actual != want:
        raise FetchError(
            f"SHA-256 mismatch for {url}\n  pinned:   {want}\n  received: {actual}\n"
            "The pinned artifact is not what the host served - this is the check that stands "
            "between a poisoned upstream and the bundle (§3.8), so the entry is NOT published."
        )
    return actual


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unpacking, confined.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def assert_confined_member(name: str, *, archive: str) -> None:
    """Reject a member path that could escape the entry root, whatever the archive claims.

    This runs IN ADDITION to the stdlib's own guards, not instead of them: `tarfile`'s `data`
    filter and `ZipFile.extractall` both sanitise, but an archive is untrusted third-party input
    and the confinement of the cache root is worth asserting where it can be read. It also runs on
    the bare-asset filename derived from the URL, which no stdlib guard covers at all.
    """
    if not name or name in (".", ".."):
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing empty or dot member path {name!r}")
    if "\x00" in name:
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing member path with an interior NUL: {name!r}")
    normalised = name.replace("\\", "/")
    if normalised.startswith("/"):
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing absolute member path {name!r}")
    parts = [p for p in normalised.split("/") if p]
    if not parts:
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing empty member path {name!r}")
    if any(p == ".." for p in parts):
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing traversing member path {name!r}")
    # A Windows drive-relative or UNC member (`C:evil`, `\\host\share`) is absolute on one
    # platform and a plain relative name on another; refuse it on every platform so an archive
    # cannot behave differently depending on which runner unpacks it.
    if len(parts[0]) >= 2 and parts[0][1] == ":":
        raise FetchError(f"{archive}: refusing drive-relative member path {name!r}")


def _archive_kind(filename: str, data: bytes) -> str:
    """Sniff the container. The NAME is a hint; the BYTES decide, so a mis-named pin unpacks
    correctly rather than landing as an opaque blob."""
    if data[:4] in (b"PK\x03\x04", b"PK\x05\x06"):
        return "zip"
    try:
        with tarfile.open(fileobj=io.BytesIO(data)):
            return "tar"
    except tarfile.TarError:
        pass
    lowered = filename.lower()
    if lowered.endswith(".zip"):
        return "zip"
    if ".tar" in lowered or lowered.endswith((".tgz", ".txz", ".tbz2")):
        return "tar"
    return "raw"


def _asset_filename(url: str) -> str:
    tail = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    return tail or "asset"


def unpack_into(data: bytes, url: str, dest: Path) -> str:
    """Materialise the asset as the per-triple tree §6.1.3 says a cache entry holds.

    Returns the container kind actually used, so the caller can report it.
    """
    dest.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    filename = _asset_filename(url)
    kind = _archive_kind(filename, data)

    if kind == "zip":
        try:
            with zipfile.ZipFile(io.BytesIO(data)) as archive:
                for name in archive.namelist():
                    assert_confined_member(name, archive=filename)
                archive.extractall(dest)
        except (zipfile.BadZipFile, OSError) as exc:
            raise FetchError(f"{filename}: unusable zip container - {exc}") from exc
    elif kind == "tar":
        try:
            with tarfile.open(fileobj=io.BytesIO(data)) as archive:
                for member in archive.getmembers():
                    assert_confined_member(member.name, archive=filename)
                    if member.islnk() or member.issym():
                        assert_confined_member(member.linkname, archive=filename)
                # `filter="data"` is the stdlib's sanctioned guard, and it models MORE than
                # assert_confined_member does - device nodes, setuid/setgid bits, absolute links.
                # Its refusals are translated here so a hostile archive exits 1 like every other
                # violation instead of escaping as an uncaught traceback.
                archive.extractall(dest, filter="data")
        except (tarfile.TarError, OSError) as exc:
            raise FetchError(f"{filename}: unusable tar container - {exc}") from exc
    else:
        # Not an archive: a bare binary or library IS the tree, stored under its own name so
        # `stage-engines`' `_member_path(entry, member)` can address it. The name comes from the
        # URL, which is untrusted in exactly the way an archive member is - a pin ending in
        # `D:evil` would otherwise collapse the join and land outside the cache root on Windows.
        assert_confined_member(filename, archive=filename)
        target = dest / filename
        if target.parent.resolve() != dest.resolve():
            raise FetchError(f"{filename}: refusing an asset name that escapes the entry root")
        target.write_bytes(data)

    if not any(dest.iterdir()):
        raise FetchError(
            f"{filename}: unpacked to an EMPTY entry - a cache entry that stages nothing would "
            "read as a silent success on every subsequent run"
        )
    return kind


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publication.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def populate(plan: FetchPlan, cache_root: Path, *, opener: Callable[..., bytes]) -> str:
    """Fetch, verify, unpack and publish ONE entry atomically. Returns the verified digest.

    Ordering is the whole point: the bytes are hashed and compared while they are still only in
    memory, the tree is built in a PRIVATE temp directory beside the destination, and the last act
    is a single `os.replace`. A failure at any step leaves the cache exactly as it was. The
    staging directory is per-ATTEMPT rather than derived from the entry name, so two concurrent
    populates of one entry cannot unpack into each other's tree.
    """
    data = fetch_asset(plan.upstream_url, opener=opener, allowed_hosts=plan.allowed_hosts)
    digest = verify_sha256(data, plan.asset_sha256, url=plan.upstream_url)

    cache_root.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    try:
        staging = Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=cache_root, prefix=f".{plan.entry_name}."))
    except OSError as exc:
        raise FetchError(
            f"{plan.entry_name}: could not create a staging directory - {exc}"
        ) from exc
    try:
        unpack_into(data, plan.upstream_url, staging)
        destination = cache_root / plan.entry_name
        try:
            os.replace(staging, destination)
        except OSError:
            # A concurrent populate winning the race is benign - its bytes verified against the
            # SAME pin. Anything else at that path is not: `.exists()` alone would also accept a
            # stray FILE, turning a permanent failure into a silent no-op reported as success.
            if not destination.is_dir():
                raise
            shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
            raise _AlreadyPublished(digest)
    except FetchError:
        shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
        raise
    except OSError as exc:
        shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
        raise FetchError(f"{plan.entry_name}: could not publish the entry - {exc}") from exc
    return digest


def run(
    *,
    lock_path: Path,
    cache_root: Path,
    triple: str,
    engine: str | None,
    check_only: bool,
    verbose: bool,
    opener: Callable[..., bytes],
) -> int:
    rows = load_lock(lock_path)
    if not rows:
        note(
            f"{lock_path.name} declares no engine rows yet - nothing to populate for {triple} "
            "(the P4.56.1 skeleton; each P5-P7 staging box adds its own row)"
        )
        return 0

    skipped: list[str] = []
    plans = select_plans(rows, engine, triple, cache_root, skipped)
    if verbose:
        for reason in skipped:
            note(f"not this path's to fetch - {reason}")
    if not plans:
        note(f"no prebuilt `engines.lock` row targets {triple} - nothing to populate")
        return 0

    hits = 0
    for plan in plans:
        members = ", ".join(plan.member_ids)
        if plan.cached:
            hits += 1
            if verbose:
                note(f"cache hit: {plan.entry_name} ({members})")
            continue
        if check_only:
            note(f"would populate: {plan.entry_name} [{members}] <- {plan.upstream_url}")
            continue
        try:
            digest = populate(plan, cache_root, opener=opener)
        except _AlreadyPublished as won:
            hits += 1
            note(
                f"{plan.entry_name} [{members}] was published by a concurrent run "
                f"(asset sha256 {won.digest[:16]}...) - ours discarded"
            )
            continue
        note(
            f"populated {plan.entry_name} [{members}] (asset sha256 {digest[:16]}...) "
            f"from {plan.upstream_url}"
        )

    verb = "would populate" if check_only else "populated"
    note(f"{triple}: {hits} cache hit(s), {verb} {len(plans) - hits} entr(ies)")
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --selftest: the fixture-driven regression suite (see the module docstring's decision note).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

_results: list[tuple[str, bool]] = []


def _record(name: str, ok: bool) -> None:
    _results.append((name, ok))


def _raises(fn, kind: type[Exception] = Exception) -> bool:
    try:
        fn()
    except kind:
        return True
    except Exception:
        return False
    return False


def _raises_saying(fn, needle: str, kind: type[Exception] = Exception) -> bool:
    """Assert the ARM, not just the type.

    Several distinct violations share one exception class, so a type-only assertion can be
    satisfied by a different arm than the one under test - which is how the malformed-pin legs
    below originally passed while the shape check they name was neutered.
    """
    try:
        fn()
    except kind as exc:
        return needle in str(exc)
    except Exception:
        return False
    return False


_LINUX = "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
_WIN = "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc"

_PAYLOAD = b"convertia-fetch-engine-assets fixture payload"
_PAYLOAD_SHA = hashlib.sha256(_PAYLOAD).hexdigest()
_HOST = "example.invalid"
# A DISTINCT host, so an allow-list leg can tell "this row's hosts" apart from "the upstream
# host only" and from "every host in the manifest".
_CORROB_HOST = "mirror.invalid"


def _row_toml(
    *,
    ident: str = "libvips",
    version: str = "8.15",
    triples: str = f'["{_LINUX}"]',
    kind: str = "staged-artifact",
    acquisition: str = "prebuilt",
    url: str = f"https://{_HOST}/libvips-8.15.tar.gz",
    asset: str | None = None,
    extra: str = "",
) -> str:
    anchor = "" if acquisition != "prebuilt" else f'asset_sha256 = "{asset or _PAYLOAD_SHA}"\n'
    return (
        "[[engine]]\n"
        f'id = "{ident}"\n'
        f'version = "{version}"\n'
        f'source_ref = "v{version}"\n'
        f"triples = {triples}\n"
        f'kind = "{kind}"\n'
        f'upstream_url = "{url}"\n'
        'licence = "LGPL-2.1-or-later"\n'
        'linkage = "invoked"\n'
        f'acquisition = "{acquisition}"\n'
        f'purl = "pkg:generic/{ident}@{version}"\n'
        f'sha256 = "{"0" * 64}"\n'
        f"{anchor}"
        f'corroboration_urls = ["https://{_CORROB_HOST}/checksums"]\n'
        f"{extra}"
    )


def _tar_bytes(members: dict[str, bytes]) -> bytes:
    buffer = io.BytesIO()
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=buffer, mode="w") as archive:
        for name, payload in members.items():
            info = tarfile.TarInfo(name)
            info.size = len(payload)
            archive.addfile(info, io.BytesIO(payload))
    return buffer.getvalue()


def _tar_device_bytes() -> bytes:
    """A tar carrying a CHARACTER-DEVICE member.

    `assert_confined_member` does not model device nodes - the stdlib `data` filter does, and
    this leg is what makes that argument checkable rather than asserted.
    """
    buffer = io.BytesIO()
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=buffer, mode="w") as archive:
        info = tarfile.TarInfo("dev/null")
        info.type = tarfile.CHRTYPE
        info.devmajor = 1
        info.devminor = 3
        archive.addfile(info)
    return buffer.getvalue()


def _tar_symlink_bytes(name: str, target: str) -> bytes:
    """A tar carrying ONE symlink member, for the linkname-confinement leg."""
    buffer = io.BytesIO()
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=buffer, mode="w") as archive:
        info = tarfile.TarInfo(name)
        info.type = tarfile.SYMTYPE
        info.linkname = target
        archive.addfile(info)
    return buffer.getvalue()


def _zip_bytes(members: dict[str, bytes]) -> bytes:
    buffer = io.BytesIO()
    with zipfile.ZipFile(buffer, mode="w") as archive:
        for name, payload in members.items():
            archive.writestr(name, payload)
    return buffer.getvalue()


class _ChunkedFake:
    """A response-shaped object that yields `total` bytes in chunks, without a socket.

    Deliberately generates rather than allocating. The ceiling legs additionally PATCH
    `MAX_ASSET_BYTES` down, because `read_capped` accumulates every chunk into one buffer - so
    feeding it a real over-ceiling body would land 1.5 GiB in the runner however cheaply the
    chunks were produced.
    """

    def __init__(self, total: int, payload: bytes | None = None) -> None:
        self._left = total
        self._payload = payload

    def remaining(self) -> int:
        """Bytes still unread - the assertion that carries \"mid-read\"."""
        return self._left

    def read(self, size: int) -> bytes:
        take = min(size, self._left)
        if take <= 0:
            return b""
        self._left -= take
        if self._payload is not None:
            start = len(self._payload) - self._left - take
            return self._payload[start : start + take]
        return b"\0" * take


def _opener_for(mapping: dict[str, bytes]) -> Callable[..., bytes]:
    def _open(url: str, allowed_hosts: frozenset[str] | None = None) -> bytes:
        if url not in mapping:
            raise AssertionError(f"the fixture opener was asked for an unpinned URL: {url}")
        return mapping[url]

    return _open


def _legs() -> None:
    # ---- lock parsing -----------------------------------------------------------------
    _record("an empty manifest is a valid container", parse_lock("# only a comment\n") == ())
    rows = parse_lock(_row_toml())
    _record("a well-formed row parses", len(rows) == 1 and rows[0].id == "libvips")
    _record("a row's triples are preserved verbatim", rows[0].triples == (_LINUX,))
    _record(
        "an unknown triple is refused",
        _raises(lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(triples='["sparc-unknown-none"]')), StructuralError),
    )
    _record(
        "an empty triples list is refused",
        _raises(lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(triples="[]")), StructuralError),
    )
    _record(
        "a missing required field is refused",
        _raises(
            lambda: parse_lock(
                _row_toml().replace(f'upstream_url = "https://{_HOST}/libvips-8.15.tar.gz"\n', "")
            ),
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a blank required field is refused",
        _raises(lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(ident="   ")), StructuralError),
    )
    _record("malformed TOML is refused", _raises(lambda: parse_lock("[[engine]\n"), StructuralError))
    _record(
        "a non-array `engine` key is refused",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock('engine = "not an array"\n'),
            "must be an array of tables",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "an `engine` entry that is not a table is refused",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock("engine = [1]\n"), "is not a table", StructuralError
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a non-array `corroboration_urls` is refused",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock(
                _row_toml().replace(
                    f'corroboration_urls = ["https://{_CORROB_HOST}/checksums"]',
                    'corroboration_urls = "one"',
                )
            ),
            # NOT the bare "must be an array": that is a substring of the `engine` arm too.
            "`corroboration_urls` must be an array",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a PREBUILT row without asset_sha256 is refused - there is nothing to verify against",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml().replace(f'asset_sha256 = "{_PAYLOAD_SHA}"\n', "")),
            "asset_sha256",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )

    # ---- the group tokens ---------------------------------------------------------------
    _record(
        "the group tokens default to the row's own id/version",
        rows[0].cache_engine == "libvips" and rows[0].cache_version == "8.15",
    )
    grouped = parse_lock(_row_toml(extra='cache_engine = "ffmpeg"\ncache_version = "7.1"\n'))
    _record(
        "a declared group token overrides the default",
        grouped[0].cache_engine == "ffmpeg" and grouped[0].cache_version == "7.1",
    )
    _record(
        "the cache key is `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`",
        cache_entry_name(grouped[0], _LINUX) == f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}",
    )
    _record(
        "an undeclared group falls back to the row key",
        cache_entry_name(rows[0], _LINUX) == f"libvips-8.15-{_LINUX}",
    )
    _record(
        "a DECLARED-but-blank group token is refused rather than silently defaulted",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(extra='cache_engine = "   "\n')),
            "declared but blank",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )

    for bad_token in ("../evil", "a/b", "a\\b", ".hidden", "", "  "):
        _record(
            f"a cache token that is not one path component is refused: {bad_token!r}",
            _raises(
                lambda b=bad_token: parse_lock(_row_toml(extra=f'cache_engine = "{b}"\n')),
                StructuralError,
            ),
        )
    _record(
        "the cache_VERSION half of the grammar is checked too, not only cache_engine",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(extra='cache_version = "../evil"\n')),
            "not usable as a cache-key path component",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a DEFAULTED token is checked too - the id is the fallback, so it is a path component",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: parse_lock(_row_toml(ident="../evil")),
            "not usable as a cache-key path component",
            StructuralError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "an ordinary versioned token passes the grammar",
        assert_safe_token("7.1-full_build", "cache_version", "w") == "7.1-full_build",
    )

    # ---- fetchability: the two REFUSED acquisition/kind shapes -------------------------
    _record("a prebuilt staged artifact is fetchable", is_fetchable(rows[0]))
    _record(
        "a from-source row is NOT fetchable",
        not is_fetchable(parse_lock(_row_toml(acquisition="from-source"))[0]),
    )
    _record(
        "a sub-component row is NOT fetchable",
        not is_fetchable(parse_lock(_row_toml(kind="sub-component"))[0]),
    )
    _record(
        "the from-source refusal names P4.28.1's compile path",
        "P4.28.1" in refusal_reason(parse_lock(_row_toml(acquisition="from-source"))[0]),
    )
    _record(
        "the sub-component refusal names the row KIND, not the acquisition mode",
        "sub-component" in refusal_reason(parse_lock(_row_toml(kind="sub-component"))[0]),
    )

    # ---- target resolution ---------------------------------------------------------------
    _record("an explicit valid triple resolves to itself", resolve_target(_WIN) == _WIN)
    _record(
        "an unknown explicit triple is a structural error",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: resolve_target("sparc-unknown-none"), "unknown target triple", StructuralError
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "this host resolves to a v1 triple, or says plainly that it cannot",
        host_target_triple() is None or host_target_triple() in V1_TARGET_TRIPLES,
    )
    _record(
        "an IMPLICIT target resolves like the host table says, or refuses in so many words",
        resolve_target(None) == host_target_triple()
        if host_target_triple() is not None
        else _raises_saying(
            lambda: resolve_target(None), "cannot resolve a target triple", StructuralError
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "every mapped host triple is a declared v1 triple",
        all(triple in V1_TARGET_TRIPLES for triple in _HOST_TRIPLES.values()),
    )

    # ---- the egress bound ----------------------------------------------------------------
    _record("an https URL is accepted", assert_fetchable_url(f"https://{_HOST}/a.tar.gz") is None)
    for scheme in ("file:///etc/passwd", f"http://{_HOST}/a", f"ftp://{_HOST}/a"):
        _record(
            f"the scheme pin refuses {scheme.split(':')[0]}://",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda s=scheme: assert_fetchable_url(s), "refusing URL scheme", FetchError
            ),
        )
    _record(
        "a hostless URL is refused",
        _raises_saying(lambda: assert_fetchable_url("https:///nowhere"), "no host", FetchError),
    )
    _record(
        "an off-allow-list host is refused even over https",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: assert_fetchable_url("https://evil.invalid/a", frozenset({_HOST})),
            "refusing host",
            FetchError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "an allow-listed host passes",
        assert_fetchable_url(f"https://{_HOST}/a", frozenset({_HOST})) is None,
    )
    _record(
        "the REAL opener carries the guard itself, not its caller",
        _raises_saying(lambda: http_opener("file:///etc/passwd"), "refusing URL scheme", FetchError),
    )
    _record(
        "the REAL opener refuses an off-allow-list host before opening a socket",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: http_opener("https://evil.invalid/a", frozenset({_HOST})),
            "refusing host",
            FetchError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "every redirect hop is re-checked - the handler refuses a downgrade to http",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: _PinnedRedirectHandler(frozenset({_HOST})).redirect_request(
                None, None, 302, "Found", {}, f"http://{_HOST}/elsewhere"
            ),
            "refusing URL scheme",
            FetchError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "every redirect hop is re-checked - the handler refuses a cross-origin hop",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: _PinnedRedirectHandler(frozenset({_HOST})).redirect_request(
                None, None, 302, "Found", {}, "https://evil.invalid/elsewhere"
            ),
            "refusing host",
            FetchError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "the per-hop pin is INSTALLED on the opener the fetch actually uses",
        any(
            isinstance(h, _PinnedRedirectHandler)
            for h in build_pinned_opener(frozenset({_HOST})).handlers
        )
        and not any(
            type(h) is urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler
            for h in build_pinned_opener(frozenset({_HOST})).handlers
        ),
    )
    # The ceiling is patched rather than fed: a real MAX_ASSET_BYTES+1 body costs ~1.6 GiB of
    # runner memory to prove one comparison, and - worse - it makes the leg unable to see the
    # regression it exists for, because a body of exactly ceiling+1 is fully consumed before
    # the bound first holds, so a check moved AFTER the loop raises the identical error.
    _ceiling = globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"]
    globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"] = 4 * 1024 * 1024
    try:
        _oversized = _ChunkedFake(globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"] * 16)
        _refused_mid_read = _raises_saying(
            lambda: read_capped(_oversized, "u"), "byte ceiling", FetchError
        )
        _left_unread = _oversized.remaining()
        _refused_after_download = _raises_saying(
            lambda: fetch_asset(
                f"https://{_HOST}/a",
                opener=lambda url, hosts=None: _ChunkedFake(
                    globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"] * 4
                ).read(globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"] * 4),
            ),
            "byte ceiling",
            FetchError,
        )
    finally:
        globals()["MAX_ASSET_BYTES"] = _ceiling
    _record(
        "the STREAMING ceiling refuses mid-read, before the whole body is buffered",
        _refused_mid_read and _left_unread > 0,
    )
    _record(
        "an oversized download is refused after the fact too, by fetch_asset's own bound",
        _refused_after_download,
    )
    _record(
        "a body under the ceiling reads back whole",
        read_capped(_ChunkedFake(len(_PAYLOAD), payload=_PAYLOAD), "u") == _PAYLOAD,
    )
    # sonnet P2: the payload-advancing arithmetic was only ever exercised at start == 0,
    # because the one real payload fits in a single chunk.
    _multi = b"".join(bytes([i % 251]) * 700_000 for i in range(4))
    _record(
        "a multi-chunk payload reads back in ORDER, not as a repeated prefix",
        read_capped(_ChunkedFake(len(_multi), payload=_multi), "u") == _multi,
    )
    _record(
        "an empty download is refused",
        _raises(
            lambda: fetch_asset(
                f"https://{_HOST}/a", opener=_opener_for({f"https://{_HOST}/a": b""})
            ),
            FetchError,
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a transport failure is reported as a fetch violation, not a traceback",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: fetch_asset(
                f"https://{_HOST}/a",
                opener=lambda url, hosts=None: (_ for _ in ()).throw(urllib.error.URLError("boom")),
            ),
            "download failed",
            FetchError,
        ),
    )

    # ---- hash verification ----------------------------------------------------------------
    _record(
        "a matching digest verifies", verify_sha256(_PAYLOAD, _PAYLOAD_SHA, url="u") == _PAYLOAD_SHA
    )
    _record(
        "an UPPERCASE pin still verifies",
        verify_sha256(_PAYLOAD, _PAYLOAD_SHA.upper(), url="u") == _PAYLOAD_SHA,
    )
    _record(
        "a mismatching digest is refused, as a MISMATCH",
        _raises_saying(
            lambda: verify_sha256(b"other", _PAYLOAD_SHA, url="u"), "mismatch", FetchError
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "a short pin is refused as MALFORMED, not as a mismatch",
        _raises_saying(lambda: verify_sha256(_PAYLOAD, "abcd", url="u"), "malformed", FetchError),
    )
    _record(
        "a non-hex pin is refused as MALFORMED, not as a mismatch",
        _raises_saying(lambda: verify_sha256(_PAYLOAD, "z" * 64, url="u"), "malformed", FetchError),
    )
    _record(
        "a 64-char pin of the WRONG bytes still reads as a mismatch",
        _raises_saying(lambda: verify_sha256(b"other", "a" * 64, url="u"), "mismatch", FetchError),
    )

    # ---- member confinement ------------------------------------------------------------
    _record("a plain member is confined", assert_confined_member("bin/vips", archive="a") is None)
    for bad in ("/etc/passwd", "../escape", "a/../../escape", "C:evil", "\\\\host\\share", ""):
        _record(
            f"a hostile member path is refused: {bad!r}",
            _raises(lambda b=bad: assert_confined_member(b, archive="a"), FetchError),
        )
    _record(
        "an interior-NUL member is refused",
        _raises(lambda: assert_confined_member("a\x00b", archive="a"), FetchError),
    )
    _record(
        "a backslash-spelled traversal is refused",
        _raises(lambda: assert_confined_member("..\\escape", archive="a"), FetchError),
    )

    # ---- archive sniffing -----------------------------------------------------------------
    _record(
        "tar magic wins over a wrong name", _archive_kind("a.bin", _tar_bytes({"x": b"y"})) == "tar"
    )
    _record(
        "zip magic wins over a wrong name", _archive_kind("a.bin", _zip_bytes({"x": b"y"})) == "zip"
    )
    _record("a bare payload is raw", _archive_kind("libvips.so", _PAYLOAD) == "raw")
    _record("an empty-zip EOCD magic is recognised", _archive_kind("a.bin", _zip_bytes({})) == "zip")
    _record(
        "a .zip NAME is the fallback when the bytes do not decide",
        _archive_kind("a.zip", b"not-an-archive") == "zip",
    )
    _record(
        "a .tar.gz NAME is the fallback when the bytes do not decide",
        _archive_kind("a.tar.gz", b"nope") == "tar",
    )
    _record("a .tgz NAME is the fallback too", _archive_kind("a.tgz", b"nope") == "tar")

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        base = Path(tmp)

        # ---- unpacking ------------------------------------------------------------------
        tar_dest = base / "tar"
        kind = unpack_into(_tar_bytes({"bin/vips": b"x"}), "https://h/a.tar", tar_dest)
        _record("a tar unpacks as a tree", kind == "tar" and (tar_dest / "bin" / "vips").is_file())

        zip_dest = base / "zip"
        kind = unpack_into(_zip_bytes({"bin/vips.exe": b"x"}), "https://h/a.zip", zip_dest)
        _record(
            "a zip unpacks as a tree", kind == "zip" and (zip_dest / "bin" / "vips.exe").is_file()
        )

        raw_dest = base / "raw"
        kind = unpack_into(_PAYLOAD, "https://h/libvips.so", raw_dest)
        _record(
            "a bare binary is stored under its own name",
            kind == "raw" and (raw_dest / "libvips.so").read_bytes() == _PAYLOAD,
        )
        _record(
            "a bare-asset NAME that escapes the entry root is refused AS a member violation",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(_PAYLOAD, "https://h/D:evil", base / "raw-escape"),
                "drive-relative",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        # A backslash-bearing URL tail is the one name that survives assert_confined_member and
        # still reaches the containment re-check: Windows reads it as a subdirectory (refused
        # there), POSIX as one legal filename (written there). The leg asserts the INVARIANT
        # both outcomes share - nothing lands outside the entry - because the re-check is
        # defence in depth over a name the member predicate has already bounded, and a leg that
        # claimed to discriminate it would be claiming more than the code can fail at.
        sep_dest = base / "raw-sep"
        sep_refused_cleanly = True
        try:
            unpack_into(_PAYLOAD, "https://h/x" + chr(92) + "y.so", sep_dest)
        except FetchError:
            pass  # Windows: the containment re-check refuses the subdirectory, CLEANLY.
        except Exception:
            # Any other type means the re-check was lost: the write then fails deep in the
            # filesystem call with a FileNotFoundError instead of this path's exit-1 contract.
            sep_refused_cleanly = False
        _record(
            "a separator in a bare-asset name is refused CLEANLY, or lands flat in the entry",
            sep_refused_cleanly
            and all(child.parent == sep_dest for child in sep_dest.rglob("*")),
        )
        _record(
            "a dot bare-asset name is refused too",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(_PAYLOAD, "https://h/a/..", base / "raw-dots"),
                "empty or dot member path",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "the stdlib data filter is applied - a device-node member is refused",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(_tar_device_bytes(), "https://h/a.tar", base / "dev-tar"),
                # `tarfile.SpecialFileError`'s own words. The generic container needle would
                # also be satisfied by the unprivileged `os.mknod` PermissionError an
                # unfiltered extract raises on a CI runner - so dropping filter="data" would
                # have stayed green on exactly the platform that runs it first.
                "is a special file",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "a truncated tar container is a violation, not a traceback",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(b"not-a-tar-at-all", "https://h/a.tar", base / "bad-tar"),
                "unusable tar container",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "a truncated zip container is a violation, not a traceback",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(b"PK\x03\x04truncated", "https://h/a.zip", base / "bad-zip"),
                "unusable zip container",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "a traversing TAR member is refused AS a traversal, before extraction",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(
                    _tar_bytes({"../escape": b"x"}), "https://h/a.tar", base / "evil-tar"
                ),
                "refusing traversing member path",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "a traversing TAR SYMLINK target is refused AS a traversal",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(
                    _tar_symlink_bytes("link", "../escape"), "https://h/a.tar", base / "evil-link"
                ),
                "refusing traversing member path",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "a traversing ZIP member is refused AS a traversal, before extraction",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: unpack_into(
                    _zip_bytes({"../escape": b"x"}), "https://h/a.zip", base / "evil-zip"
                ),
                "refusing traversing member path",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record("the refused traversal wrote NOTHING outside the entry", not (base / "escape").exists())
        _record(
            "an archive that unpacks to nothing is refused",
            _raises(
                lambda: unpack_into(_tar_bytes({}), "https://h/empty.tar", base / "empty"),
                FetchError,
            ),
        )

        # ---- grouping --------------------------------------------------------------------
        url = f"https://{_HOST}/ffmpeg-7.1-linux.tar"
        good_tar = _tar_bytes({"bin/ffmpeg": b"x", "bin/ffprobe": b"y"})
        good_sha = hashlib.sha256(good_tar).hexdigest()
        group_extra = 'cache_engine = "ffmpeg"\ncache_version = "7.1"\n'
        group_src = (
            _row_toml(ident="ffmpeg", version="7.1", url=url, asset=good_sha, extra=group_extra)
            + _row_toml(ident="ffprobe", version="7.1", url=url, asset=good_sha, extra=group_extra)
            + _row_toml(
                ident="libmp3lame.so", version="3.100", url=url, asset=good_sha, extra=group_extra
            )
        )
        group_rows = parse_lock(group_src)
        group_cache = base / "cache-group"
        plans = group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, group_cache)
        _record("three rows of one entry collapse into ONE plan", len(plans) == 1)
        _record(
            "the plan names every member it carries",
            plans[0].member_ids == ("ffmpeg", "ffprobe", "libmp3lame.so"),
        )
        _record(
            "the group is NOT keyed on the row's own version",
            plans[0].entry_name == f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"
            and any(row.version == "3.100" for row in group_rows),
        )
        forked = group_src.replace(
            f'purl = "pkg:generic/libmp3lame.so@3.100"\nsha256 = "{"0" * 64}"\n'
            f'asset_sha256 = "{good_sha}"',
            f'purl = "pkg:generic/libmp3lame.so@3.100"\nsha256 = "{"0" * 64}"\n'
            f'asset_sha256 = "{"b" * 64}"',
        )
        _record(
            "a group whose members disagree on the entry hash fails closed",
            forked != group_src
            and _raises_saying(
                lambda: group_plans(parse_lock(forked), _LINUX, group_cache),
                "ambiguous cache entry",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        forked_url = group_src.replace(
            f'id = "libmp3lame.so"\nversion = "3.100"\nsource_ref = "v3.100"\n'
            f'triples = ["{_LINUX}"]\nkind = "staged-artifact"\n'
            f'upstream_url = "{url}"',
            f'id = "libmp3lame.so"\nversion = "3.100"\nsource_ref = "v3.100"\n'
            f'triples = ["{_LINUX}"]\nkind = "staged-artifact"\n'
            f'upstream_url = "https://{_HOST}/other.tar"',
        )
        _record(
            "a group whose members disagree on the entry URL fails closed",
            forked_url != group_src
            and _raises_saying(
                lambda: group_plans(parse_lock(forked_url), _LINUX, group_cache),
                "ambiguous cache entry",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        skipped: list[str] = []
        group_plans(
            parse_lock(group_src + _row_toml(ident="libimagequant", kind="sub-component",
                                             acquisition="from-source")),
            _LINUX,
            group_cache,
            skipped,
        )
        _record(
            "a declined row is REPORTED, not silently absent",
            len(skipped) == 1 and "sub-component" in skipped[0],
        )
        _record(
            "a triple no row targets yields no plans",
            group_plans(group_rows, _WIN, group_cache) == [],
        )
        multi = parse_lock(_row_toml(triples=f'["{_LINUX}", "{_WIN}"]'))
        _record(
            "a multi-triple row plans once per triple, keyed on each",
            group_plans(multi, _LINUX, group_cache)[0].entry_name == f"libvips-8.15-{_LINUX}"
            and group_plans(multi, _WIN, group_cache)[0].entry_name == f"libvips-8.15-{_WIN}",
        )
        _record(
            "--engine selects a GROUP",
            select_plans(group_rows, "ffmpeg", _LINUX, group_cache)[0].entry_name
            == f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}",
        )
        _record(
            "--engine on an absent group is a structural error",
            _raises(lambda: select_plans(group_rows, "nope", _LINUX, group_cache), StructuralError),
        )
        _record(
            "the plan's allow-list carries the row's own hosts - upstream AND corroboration",
            plans[0].allowed_hosts == frozenset({_HOST, _CORROB_HOST}),
        )
        _record(
            "a host from ANOTHER manifest row is not on this group's allow-list",
            "other.invalid"
            not in group_plans(
                parse_lock(group_src + _row_toml(ident="pandoc", url="https://other.invalid/p.tar")),
                _LINUX,
                group_cache,
            )[0].allowed_hosts,
        )

        # ---- populate: the atomic publish ------------------------------------------------
        cache = base / "cache"
        cache.mkdir()
        plan = group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, cache)[0]
        _record("an absent entry plans as a MISS", not plan.cached)
        digest = populate(plan, cache, opener=_opener_for({url: good_tar}))
        entry = cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"
        _record(
            "populate publishes the entry under the stage-engines key",
            digest == good_sha and (entry / "bin" / "ffprobe").is_file(),
        )
        _record(
            "a published entry plans as a HIT", group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, cache)[0].cached
        )
        _record(
            "no staging residue survives a success",
            [child.name for child in cache.iterdir()] == [f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"],
        )

        bad_cache = base / "cache-bad"
        bad_cache.mkdir()
        bad_plan = group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, bad_cache)[0]
        # The payload is a VALID tar with the wrong bytes: an unusable container would satisfy a
        # type-only assertion through the container arm, so the leg has to name the arm AND deny
        # the alternative. Removing verify_sha256 outright previously left this leg green.
        _record(
            "a hash mismatch refuses to publish, AS a mismatch",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: populate(
                    bad_plan,
                    bad_cache,
                    opener=_opener_for({url: _tar_bytes({"bin/other": b"z"})}),
                ),
                "SHA-256 mismatch",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record("a refused populate leaves the cache EMPTY", not any(bad_cache.iterdir()))

        race_cache = base / "cache-race"
        race_cache.mkdir()
        # NON-empty on purpose: POSIX `rename(2)` replaces an EMPTY destination directory
        # (the call succeeds), so an empty fixture would model no race at all off Windows.
        # A real concurrent publish leaves the unpacked tree behind.
        won_entry = race_cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"
        (won_entry / "bin").mkdir(parents=True)
        (won_entry / "bin" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(b"the other run's verified bytes")
        race_plan = FetchPlan(*group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, base / "cache-race-empty")[0][:-1], cached=False)
        _record(
            "a concurrent publish is reported as such, NOT as a populate of ours",
            _raises(
                lambda: populate(race_plan, race_cache, opener=_opener_for({url: good_tar})),
                _AlreadyPublished,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "the concurrently-published entry is left INTACT and ours leaves no residue",
            [c.name for c in race_cache.iterdir()] == [f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"]
            and (won_entry / "bin" / "ffmpeg").read_bytes() == b"the other run's verified bytes",
        )
        stray_cache = base / "cache-stray"
        stray_cache.mkdir()
        (stray_cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}").write_bytes(b"not a directory")
        stray_plan = FetchPlan(*group_plans(group_rows, _LINUX, base / "cache-stray-empty")[0][:-1], cached=False)
        # Platform-split, deliberately asserted as the INVARIANT rather than as one platform's
        # outcome: `os.replace(dir, existing_file)` succeeds on Windows (the stray file is
        # replaced by our verified tree) and raises ENOTDIR on POSIX (a clean publish failure).
        # Both are correct; what must never happen is the third shape - a benign-race report that
        # leaves the stray file standing and calls it a success.
        stray_outcome: list[str] = []
        try:
            populate(stray_plan, stray_cache, opener=_opener_for({url: good_tar}))
            stray_outcome.append("published")
        except _AlreadyPublished:
            stray_outcome.append("mistaken-for-a-race")
        except FetchError:
            stray_outcome.append("failed-loudly")
        _record(
            "a stray NON-directory at the entry path is never mistaken for a benign race",
            stray_outcome != ["mistaken-for-a-race"],
        )
        _record(
            "...and it either becomes the verified tree or is left untouched by a loud failure",
            (
                stray_outcome == ["published"]
                and (stray_cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}" / "bin" / "ffmpeg").is_file()
            )
            or (
                stray_outcome == ["failed-loudly"]
                and (stray_cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}").read_bytes() == b"not a directory"
            ),
        )

        evil_tar = _tar_bytes({"../escape": b"x"})
        evil_sha = hashlib.sha256(evil_tar).hexdigest()
        evil_rows = parse_lock(_row_toml(url=url, asset=evil_sha))
        evil_cache = base / "cache-evil"
        evil_cache.mkdir()
        evil_plan = group_plans(evil_rows, _LINUX, evil_cache)[0]
        _record(
            "a hash-VALID but traversing archive is still refused AS a traversal",
            _raises_saying(
                lambda: populate(evil_plan, evil_cache, opener=_opener_for({url: evil_tar})),
                "refusing traversing member path",
                FetchError,
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "the refused traversing archive left no entry and no residue",
            not any(evil_cache.iterdir()) and not (base / "escape").exists(),
        )

        # ---- run(): the end-to-end shapes -------------------------------------------------
        empty_lock = base / "empty.lock"
        empty_lock.write_bytes(b"# no rows yet\n")
        _record(
            "an empty manifest is a no-op run",
            run(
                lock_path=empty_lock,
                cache_root=base / "cache-empty",
                triple=_LINUX,
                engine=None,
                check_only=False,
                verbose=False,
                opener=_opener_for({}),
            )
            == 0,
        )
        _record(
            "an empty manifest run creates no cache directory", not (base / "cache-empty").exists()
        )
        real_lock = base / "engines.lock"
        real_lock.write_text(group_src, encoding="utf-8")
        run_cache = base / "cache-run"
        _record(
            "--check fetches nothing",
            run(
                lock_path=real_lock,
                cache_root=run_cache,
                triple=_LINUX,
                engine=None,
                check_only=True,
                verbose=False,
                opener=_opener_for({}),
            )
            == 0,
        )
        _record("--check creates no cache directory either", not run_cache.exists())
        _record(
            "a full run populates the entry",
            run(
                lock_path=real_lock,
                cache_root=run_cache,
                triple=_LINUX,
                engine="ffmpeg",
                check_only=False,
                verbose=True,
                opener=_opener_for({url: good_tar}),
            )
            == 0
            and (run_cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}").is_dir(),
        )
        _record(
            "a second run is a pure cache hit - the opener is never called",
            run(
                lock_path=real_lock,
                cache_root=run_cache,
                triple=_LINUX,
                engine="ffmpeg",
                check_only=False,
                verbose=False,
                opener=_opener_for({}),
            )
            == 0,
        )
        # A FRESH root, and the winner publishes from INSIDE the opener: seeding the
        # destination before planning would make the plan `cached` and run() would take the
        # cache-hit branch, never reaching the handler this leg is named for.
        run_race = base / "cache-run-race"
        run_race.mkdir()
        run_race_won = run_race / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{_LINUX}"

        def _racing_opener(_url: str, _hosts: frozenset[str] | None = None) -> bytes:
            (run_race_won / "bin").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            (run_race_won / "bin" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(b"the other run's bytes")
            return good_tar

        _record(
            "run() REPORTS a concurrent publish rather than letting it escape",
            run(
                lock_path=real_lock,
                cache_root=run_race,
                triple=_LINUX,
                engine="ffmpeg",
                check_only=False,
                verbose=False,
                opener=_racing_opener,
            )
            == 0
            and (run_race_won / "bin" / "ffmpeg").read_bytes() == b"the other run's bytes",
        )
        _record(
            "a triple with no row is a no-op, not an error",
            run(
                lock_path=real_lock,
                cache_root=base / "cache-win",
                triple=_WIN,
                engine=None,
                check_only=False,
                verbose=False,
                opener=_opener_for({}),
            )
            == 0,
        )
        _record(
            "main() refuses neither --engine nor --all",
            main(["--target", _LINUX]) == 2,
        )
        _record(
            "main() refuses BOTH --engine and --all",
            main(["--target", _LINUX, "--all", "--engine", "x"]) == 2,
        )
        _record(
            "a missing manifest is a structural error",
            _raises(
                lambda: run(
                    lock_path=base / "absent.lock",
                    cache_root=base / "cache-absent",
                    triple=_LINUX,
                    engine=None,
                    check_only=False,
                    verbose=False,
                    opener=_opener_for({}),
                ),
                StructuralError,
            ),
        )


def selftest() -> int:
    try:
        _legs()
    except Exception as exc:
        # A dead tally is worse than a broad catch: the g24 canary pins the leg COUNT, so an
        # unhandled raise must surface as a NAMED failing leg rather than as a missing report.
        _record(f"the suite raised before finishing: {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}", False)

    failed = [name for name, ok in _results if not ok]
    for name in failed:
        fail(f"selftest leg failed: {name}")
    print(f"\n[fetch-engine-assets --selftest] {len(_results) - len(failed)}/{len(_results)} passed")
    return 1 if failed else 0


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="fetch-engine-assets", description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("--root", default=None, help="repo root (default: this script's repo)")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--cache-root",
        default=None,
        help=f"cache to populate (default: <root>/{DEFAULT_CACHE_REL})",
    )
    ap.add_argument("--target", default=None, help="target triple (default: this host's)")
    ap.add_argument("--engine", default=None, help="populate only this `engines.lock` cache group")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--all", action="store_true", help="populate every prebuilt entry for the target triple"
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--check", action="store_true", help="report what would be fetched; write nothing"
    )
    ap.add_argument("-v", "--verbose", action="store_true", help="report cache hits too")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--selftest", action="store_true", help="run the fixture-driven regression suite"
    )
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if args.selftest:
        return selftest()

    if (args.engine is None) == (not args.all):
        fail("pass exactly one of --engine <group> or --all")
        return 2

    root = Path(args.root).resolve() if args.root else ROOT
    cache_root = Path(args.cache_root).resolve() if args.cache_root else root / DEFAULT_CACHE_REL

    try:
        triple = resolve_target(args.target)
        return run(
            lock_path=root / LOCK_REL,
            cache_root=cache_root,
            triple=triple,
            engine=args.engine,
            check_only=args.check,
            verbose=args.verbose,
            opener=http_opener,
        )
    except StructuralError as exc:
        fail(str(exc))
        return 2
    except FetchError as exc:
        fail(str(exc))
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
